\documentclass[11pt]{article}

\usepackage[review]{acl}

\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{caption}

\title{Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Linear Recurrent Architectures}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Practitioners select among hybrid linear recurrent architectures using aggregate benchmarks, but these scores can mask per-example consequences. Across three controlled designs, adding attention affects substantially different examples per architecture (Jaccard $\approx$ 0.10, approximately twice the independence baseline); validation across five architectures and ten pairwise comparisons confirms this on all four benchmarks (all $p < .01$). Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid} (three architectures, five attention ratios, two scales), we decompose per-example effects and find that this predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} depends on task type ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) rather than attention dose. Aggregate trends do not reach statistical significance ($n = 5$; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$), yet per-example overlap saturates at 4\% attention and remains flat across all four benchmarks. Majority vote fails to exploit this complementarity, confirming that configurations interchangeable by aggregate metrics carry hidden per-example consequences. Confidence-informed routing extracts 36\% of oracle complementarity on LAMBADA ($+5.3$\,pp), suggesting these differences are partially actionable.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Single training runs.} Each configuration corresponds to a single training run, so we cannot fully disentangle architectural from seed-specific effects. The $3\times$ within- vs.\ cross-architecture Jaccard gap and Pythia 70M seed calibration (within-seed $J$ is 2.7--4.3$\times$ higher; Appendix~\ref{app:seed_calibration}) provide proxy evidence, and the consistency of significant Jaccard across all 40 architecture--benchmark combinations (all $p < .01$) would be unlikely under seed noise alone. With $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable $p$ is 0.017, so LAMBADA's Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$ does not reach conventional significance.

\paragraph{Coverage.} We examine three primary architectures (plus two extended) from one model zoo, four main benchmarks, and two scale points. Generalization to Mamba, RWKV, other hybrid families, and generation tasks remains open.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All evaluations use pretrained checkpoints without fine-tuning; per-example effects may not persist after task-specific adaptation.

\section*{Code and Data Availability}

All models are from the publicly available model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis code and per-example evaluation outputs will be released upon publication.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Conclusion}

Per-example multiplicity in hybrid recurrent models varies by task ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$), with Jaccard uniformly low ($\leq 0.10$) even as aggregate metrics converge on LAMBADA (dependent on GDN inclusion; \S\ref{ssec:robustness}); five-architecture validation confirms this across four benchmarks (all $J \leq 0.14$, $p < .01$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_arch}). Pure-model confidence partially predicts affected examples (AUC 0.70--0.74; \S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

For practitioners, three implications follow from the ${\sim}90\%$ non-overlap: (1) $\alpha > 0.2$ warrants per-example audit before architecture substitution, as multiplicity at this level means $>$20\% of examples change outcome; (2) low-Jaccard pairs maximize ensemble complementarity---majority vote fails despite 15--30\% disagreement, but oracle accuracy reveals $+7$--$14$\,pp of untapped gain; (3) rescue-threshold routing is viable when $\alpha$ is high and disruption thresholds differ across architectures, extracting 36\% of oracle complementarity on LAMBADA ($+5.3$\,pp; \S\ref{ssec:per_example}). Deployment-ready routing remains open.

\section{Introduction}

Hybrid recurrent architectures that appear equivalent by aggregate benchmarks affect largely non-overlapping sets of individual examples: adding attention to three controlled linear recurrent backbones yields helped and hurt sets with at most 10\% pairwise overlap, approximately twice the independence baseline. This per-example divergence is invisible in aggregate metrics: on LAMBADA, cross-architecture standard deviation drops 79\% as attention increases (Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$, BCa CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$), but each architecture reaches similar accuracy by gaining and losing accuracy on different inputs. Practitioners increasingly choose between recurrent backbones based on aggregate benchmark scores. Across all ten pairwise comparisons among five architectures, overlap in per-example effects remains below 14\% (all $p < .01$), meaning the choice of backbone is associated with which specific inputs a deployed system handles correctly.

This tension arises in the context of a rapid architectural shift toward hybrid recurrent-attention models, which interleave attention layers within linear recurrent backbones. Jamba \citep{lieber2024jamba}, Griffin \citep{de2024griffin}, RWKV-6 \citep{peng2024eagle}, and RecurrentGemma \citep{botev2024recurrentgemma} each combine recurrence with attention using different mixing strategies. These systems now serve as production-grade alternatives to pure Transformers, and the practical question of how much attention to include is typically answered by comparing aggregate benchmark numbers. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide a controlled model zoo spanning three recurrent architectures, five attention ratios, and two parameter scales, which enables systematic study of how attention allocation affects these hybrid designs.

Most evaluations of hybrid architectures compare aggregate accuracy, which can mask per-example behavioral diversity. Prior work on predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} and underspecification \citep{damour2022underspecification,mccoy2020berts} documents this risk for seed variation within a single architecture, but whether the phenomenon extends to architectural variation in hybrid recurrent models remains open. If architectures that score identically in aggregate behave differently on individual inputs, the choice of backbone carries hidden consequences for deployment reliability and ensemble construction.

We present the first systematic per-example analysis of how attention affects cross-architecture agreement in hybrid recurrent models. Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, we compare three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios (0\% to 25\%) and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). These three architectures represent distinct approaches to linear recurrence: gated delta rules, multi-scale exponential decay, and gated state expansion. We evaluate all 30 configurations on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories. On four benchmarks (LAMBADA, $N = 5{,}153$; HellaSwag, $N = 10{,}042$; PIQA, $N = 1{,}838$; ARC-Easy, $N = 2{,}376$) we conduct per-example decompositions, classifying each example as helped, hurt, or unchanged when attention is added, and measure cross-architecture overlap using Jaccard similarity. Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling} previews our central result: aggregate convergence and per-example divergence follow different trajectories as attention ratio increases.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_decoupling_hero.pdf}
\caption{Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled (helped: correct only with attention; hurt: correct only without). (a) Cross-architecture standard deviation on LAMBADA decreases gradually with attention ratio at 1.3B (solid) but partially rebounds at 340M (dashed). (b) Cross-architecture pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped/hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat. Shaded bands are illustrative ($n = 3$).}
\label{fig:decoupling}
\end{figure}

Our analysis yields two findings. First, per-example predictive multiplicity varies in magnitude across tasks, forming a task-dependent variation. The ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive} ranges from 0.14 (PIQA) through 0.15 (HellaSwag) and 0.21 (ARC-Easy) to 0.30 (LAMBADA), while pairwise Jaccard remains uniformly low ($\leq 0.10$, all $p < .01$). Within each benchmark, $\alpha$ is stable across attention ratios (variation $< 0.03$), but differs substantially across task types, ranging from 0.14 (reasoning) to 0.30 (retrieval).

Aggregate convergence is task-specific and exploratory: of 10 benchmarks, only LAMBADA shows monotonic variance reduction ($\rho = -1.0$, Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$; observed at 1.3B, absent at 340M). Yet on this benchmark, per-example Jaccard saturates at the first attention level while aggregate variance continues to decrease (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}).

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We show that architectural variation under matched training conditions is associated with structured per-example predictive multiplicity ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) across four benchmarks, with magnitude determined by task type rather than attention dose (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}, Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}). Extended validation across five architectures confirms the pattern: all ten pairwise Jaccard values remain $\leq 0.14$ across four benchmarks (all $p < .01$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_arch}). The primary controlled variable is the recurrent backbone \citep[cf.][]{mccoy2020berts, damour2022underspecification}, though single training runs prevent full disentanglement from seed effects (Limitations).
    \item Per-example Jaccard saturates at the first attention level (4\%) across all four benchmarks and shows no further increase at higher ratios, while aggregate variance shows a non-significant decreasing pattern on LAMBADA (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_bench_jaccard}). This immediate saturation indicates that minimal attention suffices to establish the full pattern of configuration-specific per-example effects; additional attention narrows aggregate gaps without homogenizing which examples each architecture affects. The aggregate convergence component is dependent on GDN inclusion (\S\ref{ssec:robustness}) and does not replicate on other benchmarks.
\end{itemize}

